    prov:endedAtTime "2026-08-10T20:05:07Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr449Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #360 asked for turtle::convert_literal to stop collapsing \"...\"^^xsd:string literals into RdfLiteral::LiteralString at ingestion, so STRDT()/STRLANG() could spec-correctly reject genuinely xsd:string-typed input (the W3C strdt03/strlang03 fixtures). Before touching any code, I grepped RdfLiteral usage across the workspace as instructed, then wrote a throwaway program constructing oxrdf::Literal::new_simple_literal(\"foo\") and Literal::new_typed_literal(\"foo\", xsd::STRING) directly and compared them: they are the same LiteralContent variant, is_plain() is true for both, and a == b is true. That is decisive: oxttl's Turtle grammar builds every ^^<iri> literal via new_typed_literal, so the distinction the issue wanted preserved is destroyed inside the oxrdf dependency before turtle::convert_literal's collapsing branch ever runs. Removing that branch, as the issue title asked, would not fix anything -- it would only start mistagging every bare \"foo\" (no datatype written at all) as TypedLiteral{xsd:string}, since lit.datatype() also reports xsd:string for those. I confirmed oxttl exposes no token/span API to recover the original written form, so recovering it would mean hand-rolling a parallel Turtle/TriG/N-Triples/N-Quads literal tokenizer kept in permanent lockstep with oxttl's own grammar -- assessed as materially riskier than the narrow fix #346 already declined to attempt, and I did not attempt it. I also worked out why even a successful recovery would be dangerous on its own: dag_rdf::GraphElementManager interns literals into an exact-key HashMap, and eval_triple_pattern_core gates on that same map, so a stored TypedLiteral{xsd:string,\"foo\"} would silently stop matching a plain-literal query pattern \"foo\" -- a join-engine regression, not just a storage one. Given that, I left turtle::convert_literal untouched and rewrote the tests/w3c_sparql11_suite.rs skip-list comment to document the oxrdf-level finding instead of the original (now known incorrect) explanation, and wrote up both findings in docs/plans/XSD_STRING_LITERAL_360_PLAN.md so the scope decision (close #360 as not-representable-by-this-parser-stack, vs. retitle toward the much larger value-based-interning-plus-custom-tokenizer follow-up) is Dag's to make. Along the way I found that sparql_parser's own query-text literal grammar (parse_string_literal) already keeps \"foo\"^^xsd:string distinct from \"foo\", unlike Turtle ingestion -- so FILTER(\"foo\" = \"foo\"^^xsd:string) was already false today with no Turtle involved at all, which is exactly the RDF 1.1 value-equivalence regression the task brief said must not happen. I fixed that real, in-scope, low-risk bug: values_equal now normalizes LiteralString against TypedLiteral{xsd:string} the same way it already normalizes numeric/boolean cross-representation mismatches from #208, with tests (initially #[ignore]d, confirmed red via --ignored, then unignored) covering both operand orders, !=, IN/NOT IN, and negative guards that language-tagged literals and differing lexical values are not swept into the same equality." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:449 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr449Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr449 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr449Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Left turtle::convert_literal unchanged; fixed only the unrelated-but-real values_equal string/xsd:string equality gap in sparql_parser, and documented the oxrdf-level blocker in the plan doc for a maintainer scope decision on #360 itself." ;
    agp:alternative "Editing convert_literal to drop its xsd:string collapse branch, per the issue's literal title. Rejected once direct construction proved oxrdf::Literal already collapses xsd:string typing at the constructor level (new_simple_literal(\"foo\") == new_typed_literal(\"foo\", xsd::STRING) is true) -- the edit would not recover the distinction and would misclassify every untyped plain literal as xsd:string-typed instead, a strictly worse outcome for zero benefit." .
